{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792842054,"nonce":"11Cai1gEB28Ok1N4UwkZcsRVTlo=","title":"Session ids and CSRF tokens are stored in plaintext in Postgres","message":"`crates/scarab-db-postgres/migrations/0025_sessions.sql` stores the session id (the bearer secret behind the `scarab_session` cookie and CLI `Bearer` use) and its CSRF token in plaintext. A database read — a backup, a replica, a leaked dump or a read-only SQL injection — is therefore enough to ride any live session.\n\nWhy it matters more now (ADR-0073 step-up): the new step-up tables key windows by `session_hash = sha256(session id)` and seal a Bearer requester's session id, but a DB reader can take the raw id straight from `sessions` and:\n- use any session that has an open 5-minute StepUpConfirmation window to run window-mode crown-jewel operations;\n- enroll their own FIRST passkey on a passkey-less admin whose session is still inside the 5-minute fresh-login window.\n\nFound by the ADR-0073 safety review (finding S3, review/safety.md in AFK run 20261024-1301). Pre-existing; deliberately not fixed in the step-up build.\n\nSuggested fix: store only `sha256(session id)` as the key (look up by hash) and either derive the CSRF token from the id with an HMAC or store it hashed too; forward migration that hashes existing rows (or invalidates them, forcing re-login).","files":null}]}